I have a table full of stock and option positions (an "option position" is a combination of option expiration date and strike price). The columns: userID - the person who owns the position stock_symbol - the stock they own call_expiration - expiration date for a call option they've sold against the stock call_strike - strike price for the same call option
Users can have multiple stock positions for the same stock_symbol, and if they do then they may have different call options written against that same symbol. For example user 399 has 3 positions in AAPL, each with a different call option: + + + + + | user_id | stock_symbol | call_expiration | call_strike | + + + + + | 399 | AAPL | 2018-07-20 | 185 | | 399 | AAPL | 2018-07-20 | 190 | | 399 | AAPL | 2018-08-17 | 180 | | 399 | X | 2018-07-20 | 13 | | 802 | AAPL | 2018-07-20 | 190 | | 802 | X | 2019-01-18 | 14 | | 802 | MU | 2018-07-20 | 38 | | 802 | MIC | 2018-07-20 | 42.5 | | 802 | AAPL | 2018-07-20 | 190 | | 1079 | MU | 2018-08-17 | 39 | | 1079 | X | 2018-07-20 | 14 |
I want to know which stocks are most commonly held, but I want each user to have 1 vote (in the above data symbol X wins because 3 users have a position in it; AAPL has more positions but they are owned by only 2 users). So if the same user has multiple positions in the same stock_symbol then it only counts as 1 vote for that stock_symbol. If there is a tie in frequency then order alphabetically by symbol. Maybe there's an easier way but this works: SELECT stock_symbol, COUNT(*) AS symbol_count FROM (SELECT DISTINCT(user_id), stock_symbol FROM user_positions) AS temp GROUP BY stock_symbol ORDER BY symbol_count DESC, stock_symbol ASC LIMIT 10;
Then I wanted to know which expiration date was the most common for each of the popular symbols. Here's how I'd find it for a hard-coded stock_symbol (AAPL). If there is a tie then the earliest expiration date wins: SELECT call_expiration, COUNT(*) AS exp_count FROM positions WHERE stock_symbol='AAPL' GROUP BY call_expiration ORDER BY exp_count DESC, call_expiration ASC;
Then, lastly, I'd like to know the most common strike_price for a given symbol and expiration date. Here's how I'd find it for a hard coded symbol and expiration date. If there is a tie then the lowest strike price wins: SELECT call_strike, COUNT(*) AS strike_count FROM positions WHERE stock_symbol='AAPL' AND call_expiration = '2018-07-20' GROUP BY call_strike ORDER BY strike_count DESC, call_strike ASC;
These work and I can put these calls into a php script and loop through the results of the first query (which generated the 10 most commonly held stock_symbols), but I'm wondering if there's a way to get mysql to do all of this for me.
The end goal is a table like this: popular position #1: S1 E1 K1 popular position #2: S2 E2 K2 popular position #3: S3 E3 K3
Where: S1 = the most commonly held stock_symbol when each user gets 1 vote E1 = the most common expiration date across all positions for symbol S1 (if a user has multiple positions for that symbol then you can consider all of them) K1 = the most common call_strike for all positions where the symbol and expiraiton date are fixed at S1 and E1. (if a user has multiple positions for that symbol then you can consider all of them)
I'm not sure if this is a bad idea or not but I thought of creating intermediate columns in my query that concat call_expiration+call_strike, or maybe all 3: symbol+expiration+strike; couldn't quite figure it out since in the first case (most popular symbols) I want users to only have 1 vote each, but in the next 2 queries (most popular expiration date and most popular strike price for that expiration date) it's okay to consider all of a user's positions, even if they have more than 1 for the same symbol. Maybe too much to do in a single query?
